Switch: tell a slow warm-up build that waited for the GL context from a shader cache miss

On every warm boot (Mesa's shader cache and Dawn's program binaries complete: 1576 of 1576 nvc0
cache hits, 787 of 787 binaries, 0 compiles) the 3-4 warm-up builds that ran during the game's
first frame took 70-130 ms each instead of ~11 ms: the render worker held the one GL context for
~310 ms in that frame, and a build's time counted its wait for the context. The harness took them
for cache misses and brought the loading screen up for the whole warm-up.

With Dawn's per-thread build split (switch/dawn/patches/dawn-switch-gl-pipeline-wait.patch):
- AuroraSwitchPrecompile::contextWaitNs: of compileNs, the warm-up's wait for the GL context;
- the throttle holds the next warm-up build back only after a build slow for its own work (its
  time minus the wait) - a build that only waited is no cache miss;
- aurora_switch_get_slow_builds: the last 32 builds longer than the slow threshold, with their
  pipeline key and where the time went (translation, context wait, context held), for the
  "[cos] precompile slow build" lines.

Switch only (switch/native/aurora/patches); not for upstream.

--- a/include/aurora/switch_precompile.h
+++ b/include/aurora/switch_precompile.h
